THINKERS.push(
{
  id: 'spinoza', name: `Baruch Spinoza`, born: 1632, died: 1677, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Amsterdam; The Hague`, role: `Philosopher and lens grinder`,
  themes: ['god', 'mind', 'ethics', 'suffering'], keywords: ['ethics', 'god or nature', 'deus sive natura', 'determinism', 'conatus', 'affects', 'emotions', 'pantheism', 'excommunication', 'freedom'],
  tagline: `God and Nature are the same thing, and freedom means understanding why you do what you do.`,
  summary: `Spinoza argued that there is only one substance, which you can call God or Nature. Everything, including you, is a part or expression of it, and everything happens by necessity. There is no free will in the ordinary sense. But there is freedom of another kind: by understanding the causes of our emotions, we stop being their slaves. His *Ethics*, written in the form of geometric proofs, ends in a calm, clear love of the whole of reality.`,
  life: `Baruch (Bento, later Benedict) de Spinoza was born in 1632 in Amsterdam, into a family of Portuguese Jews whose ancestors had been forced to convert to Christianity in Portugal and who had fled the Inquisition to the relatively tolerant Dutch Republic, where they could practise Judaism openly again. His father was a successful merchant. Spinoza received a traditional Jewish education and was a gifted student.

He also started reading Descartes, the new science and radical thinkers, and developed ideas the community found intolerable. On 27 July 1656, aged 23, he was issued a *cherem*, a writ of excommunication, of unusual severity: “Cursed be he by day and cursed be he by night; cursed be he when he lies down, and cursed be he when he rises up.” No one was to communicate with him, stay under the same roof, or read anything he wrote. The document doesn’t specify his offences, only “abominable heresies” and “monstrous deeds”. He never returned.

He changed his name to the Latin Benedictus, which, like Baruch, means “blessed”. He supported himself by grinding lenses for microscopes and telescopes, a skilled trade at which he was respected; the glass dust may have worsened the lung disease that killed him. He lived modestly in rented rooms in villages near Leiden and then in The Hague. He turned down a professorship at Heidelberg in 1673, worried it would limit his freedom to think. He turned down a pension offered on condition he dedicate a book to Louis XIV.

In 1670 he published the *Theological-Political Treatise* anonymously. It argued that the Bible should be read as a historical document, written by many human authors, and that the state should allow freedom of thought and speech. It caused an uproar and was banned. His masterpiece, the *Ethics*, he decided not to publish in his lifetime, fearing the reaction.

In 1672, when a mob in The Hague lynched the brothers Johan and Cornelis de Witt, the republican leaders, Spinoza, usually calm, wrote a sign reading *ultimi barbarorum*, “the lowest of barbarians”, and wanted to post it at the scene. His landlord locked him in the house to save his life.

He died in 1677, aged 44, probably of tuberculosis or silicosis. His friends published the *Ethics* later that year, without his name on the title page.`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `God or Nature`, b: `Spinoza starts from the idea of substance: something that exists in itself and is conceived through itself, depending on nothing else. He argues that there can only be one such substance, and that it must be infinite. He calls it “God, or Nature” (*Deus sive Natura*).

Everything that exists (you, stars, thoughts, stones) is a “mode” of this one substance: a way that God-or-Nature exists and expresses itself. God isn’t a person outside the world who created it and watches over it. God is the world, understood in its totality and its necessity.

This God has no emotions, no plans and no favourites. It doesn’t reward or punish. It doesn’t act for purposes. Spinoza thought the idea of a God who made everything for human benefit was a projection of human vanity. He was called an atheist for this, and alternatively a “God-intoxicated man” (by the poet Novalis). Einstein, asked if he believed in God, said: “I believe in Spinoza’s God, who reveals himself in the orderly harmony of what exists.”` },
    { t: `Everything is necessary`, b: `In nature there is nothing contingent; everything is determined to exist and act in a certain way by the necessity of the divine nature. There are no accidents. There is also no free will in the ordinary sense. People think they are free only because they are conscious of their desires but ignorant of the causes that determine them. A stone thrown through the air, if it were conscious, would think it was flying of its own free will.

This sounds bleak. Spinoza thought it was the opposite: understanding necessity is the beginning of peace.` },
    { t: `Mind and body are one thing`, b: `Against Descartes, Spinoza held that mind and body aren’t two substances. They are one and the same thing, conceived in two different ways: once under the attribute of thought, once under the attribute of extension (space). Every bodily event has a mental side and every mental event a bodily side. This neatly dissolves Descartes’s problem of how the mind moves the body: it doesn’t, because they aren’t two things. Many neuroscientists, including Antonio Damasio in *Looking for Spinoza*, have found this remarkably modern.` },
    { t: `Conatus`, b: `“Each thing, as far as it lies in itself, strives to persevere in its being.” This striving, the *conatus*, is the actual essence of every thing. For humans, it takes the form of desire. And Spinoza turns traditional morality upside down: we don’t desire things because they are good; we call them good because we desire them.` },
    { t: `The emotions, analysed like geometry`, b: `Spinoza said he would treat human emotions “as if it were a question of lines, planes and bodies”. Every emotion is built from three basic ones: desire, joy (the passage to greater power of acting) and sadness (the passage to lesser). Love is joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause; hatred is sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. From these he derives dozens of others: hope, fear, envy, pity, pride, shame.

The analysis is often uncannily accurate. Hope and fear always come together, because hope is joy mixed with doubt about the outcome, and where there is doubt there is fear. Envy is hatred that makes a person sad at another’s happiness and glad at their misfortune.` },
    { t: `Human bondage and freedom`, b: `Part IV of the *Ethics* is called “Of Human Bondage, or the Power of the Emotions”. We are in bondage when we are driven by passions: emotions caused by outside things we don’t understand. A person in their grip “is not his own master”, often seeing the better and doing the worse.

The way out is understanding. “An emotion that is a passion ceases to be a passion as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea of it.” When you understand why you feel what you feel (the causes, the necessity, the chain behind it), the emotion changes from something done to you into something you understand, and you become more active. You can’t decide not to be angry. But understanding the causes of your anger weakens its hold. This is not far from modern psychotherapy.` },
    { t: `Seeing things under the aspect of eternity`, b: `The highest form of knowledge, which Spinoza calls intuitive knowledge, sees each thing as following necessarily from God-or-Nature: *sub specie aeternitatis*, under the aspect of eternity. From this comes the “intellectual love of God”, a calm, unshakeable joy in understanding reality as it is. It isn’t a love that expects to be loved back: “He who loves God cannot strive that God should love him in return.”

The *Ethics* ends: “If the way I have shown to lead to these things now seems very hard, still, it can be found. And of course, what is found so rarely must be hard. For if salvation were at hand and could be found without great effort, how could nearly everyone neglect it? But all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.”` },
    { t: `Freedom of thought`, b: `In the *Theological-Political Treatise*, Spinoza made one of the first great arguments for freedom of speech and thought. No one can transfer their right to think to anyone else. A state that tries to control people’s beliefs creates hypocrites and rebels. “The purpose of the state is freedom.”` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `Ethics, Demonstrated in Geometrical Order`, y: `1677, posthumous`, b: `Five parts: on God, on the mind, on the emotions, on human bondage, and on human freedom. Written like Euclid, with definitions, axioms, propositions, proofs and corollaries. This makes it look impossibly dry at first. The secret is that the best parts are in the “scholia” (explanatory notes) and prefaces, where Spinoza speaks directly and sometimes passionately. Start with the Appendix to Part I and the Preface to Part IV.` },
    { t: `Theological-Political Treatise`, y: `1670`, b: `The founding text of modern biblical criticism and a powerful case for secular government and free speech.` },
    { t: `Treatise on the Emendation of the Intellect`, y: `unfinished`, b: `Opens with a gorgeous passage on why he turned from wealth, honour and pleasure to seek a lasting good. An excellent entry point.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `All things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.`, s: `*Ethics* V, last line` },
    { q: `A free man thinks of nothing less than of death, and his wisdom is a meditation not on death but on life.`, s: `*Ethics* IV, Proposition 67` },
    { q: `I have striven not to laugh at human actions, not to weep at them, nor to hate them, but to understand them.`, s: `*Political Treatise* I.4` },
    { q: `An emotion that is a passion ceases to be a passion as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea of it.`, s: `*Ethics* V, Proposition 3` },
    { q: `Men think themselves free because they are conscious of their volitions and desires, and are ignorant of the causes by which they are led to wish and desire.`, s: `*Ethics* I, Appendix` },
    { q: `We do not desire something because we judge it to be good; on the contrary, we judge something to be good because we desire it.`, s: `*Ethics* III, Proposition 9, scholium` },
    { q: `The purpose of the state is really freedom.`, s: `*Theological-Political Treatise*, chapter 20` }
  ],
  fakes: [
    { q: `If you want the present to be different from the past, study the past.`, n: `Widely attributed, but no source in his works has been found.` },
    { q: `Peace is not the absence of war; it is a virtue, a state of mind, a disposition for benevolence, confidence, justice.`, n: `A loose, expanded version of a line in the *Political Treatise* (V.4). The original is shorter and is specifically about a state’s condition.` }
  ],
  critique: `The geometric method promises certainty it can’t deliver. The definitions at the start of Part I do a huge amount of work, and if you don’t accept them, the whole system doesn’t follow. Critics from Leibniz on have found gaps in the proofs.

Determinism raises the familiar problem. If everything is necessary and there is no free will, what sense does it make to tell people to become free, or to praise and blame them? Spinoza’s answer is that the “freedom” he recommends is itself caused (by understanding), but many readers feel this redefines freedom out of existence.

His God is also hard to recognise as God. Something that has no will, no love for individuals and no purposes is, to most religious believers, simply nature, and calling it God looks like a way of avoiding the charge of atheism. His Jewish community and Christian critics said exactly that.

Finally, the ideal of calm understanding can seem cold. Spinoza says that pity, humility and repentance are not virtues. Is it really wise to aim at not being moved by others’ suffering, but only to act to relieve it, rationally?`,
  start: `Read Steven Nadler’s *Spinoza: A Life* for the man, and Nadler’s *Think Least of Death* (2020) for a clear account of his ethics as a guide to living. Then try the *Ethics* in Edwin Curley’s translation (Penguin), starting with Part I’s Appendix, then Parts III–V. Don’t worry about following every proof on a first read.`,
  related: ['descartes', 'marcus-aurelius', 'nietzsche', 'hegel', 'freud']
},
{
  id: 'locke', name: `John Locke`, born: 1632, died: 1704, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Wrington, Somerset; Oxford; London; the Netherlands`, role: `Philosopher and physician`,
  themes: ['politics', 'knowledge', 'mind'], keywords: ['blank slate', 'tabula rasa', 'natural rights', 'life liberty property', 'consent', 'toleration', 'empiricism', 'personal identity', 'two treatises'],
  tagline: `Governments exist to protect your rights, and if they don’t, you can replace them.`,
  summary: `Locke argued that all our knowledge comes from experience (the mind at birth is a blank slate), that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property, and that governments get their authority only from the consent of the governed. A government that violates those rights can legitimately be overthrown. These ideas went straight into the American Declaration of Independence and the foundations of liberal democracy.`,
  life: `John Locke was born in 1632 in Somerset, the son of a lawyer who fought for Parliament in the English Civil War. He went to Westminster School and Christ Church, Oxford, where he found the traditional Aristotelian curriculum dull. He was drawn instead to the new experimental science and to medicine, and worked with the chemist Robert Boyle.

In 1666 he met Lord Ashley, later Earl of Shaftesbury, and became his physician and adviser. In 1668 he supervised a risky operation to drain a liver abscess, and the silver tube he had inserted stayed in Shaftesbury for the rest of his life. Shaftesbury became one of the most powerful men in England, and then a leader of the opposition to the Catholic heir to the throne, James. When Shaftesbury’s plotting failed, he fled to Holland in 1682, where he died. Locke, under suspicion, followed him into exile in 1683, living under a false name for a time.

He returned to England in 1689, after the Glorious Revolution had replaced James II with William and Mary, and quickly published the books he had been working on for years: the *Essay Concerning Human Understanding*, the *Two Treatises of Government* (anonymously) and the *Letter Concerning Toleration*. They made him famous. He spent his last years at the country house of his friend Damaris Masham, a philosopher herself, and died in 1704.`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `The blank slate`, b: `Against Descartes, who believed some ideas (like God) are innate, Locke argued that the mind at birth is like “white paper, void of all characters”. Everything we know comes from experience, either from **sensation** (the senses) or from **reflection** (observing the operations of our own minds). Complex ideas are built up from simple ones. This is empiricism, and it shaped British philosophy for the next two centuries.` },
    { t: `Personal identity`, b: `What makes you the same person over time? Not the same body, Locke argued. Imagine a prince’s soul, with all his memories, entering the body of a cobbler: we would say it is the prince in a cobbler’s body. Personal identity consists in consciousness, specifically memory: you are the same person as someone in the past if you can remember their experiences as your own. The theory had problems (Thomas Reid pointed out that you might remember being a boy, and an old man remember being an officer, but not the boy), but it launched the modern debate.` },
    { t: `Natural rights`, b: `Before government, in the state of nature, people live under the law of nature, which reason teaches: since we are all equal and independent, no one ought to harm another in their life, health, liberty or possessions. These are natural rights. We don’t get them from governments; governments exist to protect them.` },
    { t: `Property and labour`, b: `How can anyone own anything, if God gave the earth to everyone in common? Locke’s answer: you own your own body, and so you own the labour of your body. When you mix your labour with something (pick an apple, plough a field), you make it yours, as long as you leave “enough and as good” for others and don’t let it spoil. This labour theory of property was enormously influential and has been criticised just as much.` },
    { t: `Consent and revolution`, b: `People leave the state of nature and form governments to protect their rights more securely. So the legitimate power of government comes only from the consent of the governed, and it is limited to the purposes for which it was created. When a government violates the rights it was set up to protect, it puts itself into a state of war with the people, who may resist and replace it. This was the justification for the Glorious Revolution of 1688 and later for the American Revolution.` },
    { t: `Toleration`, b: `The state’s job is to protect civil interests (life, liberty, health, property), not to save souls. Belief can’t be forced; torture can make someone say they believe but not actually believe. So the state should tolerate different religions. Locke’s toleration had limits, though: he excluded Catholics (whom he thought owed allegiance to a foreign prince, the Pope) and atheists (whose oaths couldn’t be trusted).` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `An Essay Concerning Human Understanding`, y: `1689`, b: `Four books on the origins and limits of human knowledge. Long and repetitive, but clearly written. Book II on ideas and the chapter on personal identity (II.27) are the most important.` },
    { t: `Two Treatises of Government`, y: `1689`, b: `The first attacks Robert Filmer’s argument that kings rule by divine right inherited from Adam; nobody reads it now. The second sets out Locke’s own theory and is one of the founding texts of liberalism.` },
    { t: `A Letter Concerning Toleration`, y: `1689`, b: `Short, clear and still relevant.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `Let us then suppose the mind to be, as we say, white paper, void of all characters, without any ideas.`, s: `*Essay* II.1.2` },
    { q: `Wherever law ends, tyranny begins.`, s: `*Second Treatise*, §202` },
    { q: `The end of law is not to abolish or restrain, but to preserve and enlarge freedom.`, s: `*Second Treatise*, §57` },
    { q: `Being all equal and independent, no one ought to harm another in his life, health, liberty or possessions.`, s: `*Second Treatise*, §6` },
    { q: `New opinions are always suspected, and usually opposed, without any other reason but because they are not already common.`, s: `*Essay*, Epistle Dedicatory` }
  ],
  fakes: [],
  critique: `Locke’s ideals of liberty and equality sat alongside some deep compromises. He invested in the Royal African Company, which traded in enslaved people, and helped write the *Fundamental Constitutions of Carolina* (1669), which gave colonists “absolute power and authority” over their slaves. His labour theory of property was used to justify taking land from Native Americans, on the grounds that they hadn’t “improved” it by farming in the European way.

Philosophically, the social contract is a fiction: no one alive actually consented to their government. Locke’s answer, “tacit consent” (you consent by living in a country and using its roads), is weak; David Hume compared it to saying that a man carried aboard a ship while asleep has consented to the captain’s authority because he doesn’t jump into the sea.

And the “blank slate” is now known to be wrong. Babies are born with a great deal of built-in structure, as Steven Pinker argues in *The Blank Slate*.`,
  start: `Read the *Second Treatise of Government* (Hackett, ed. C. B. Macpherson), about 120 pages, and the chapter on personal identity from the *Essay* (II.27). John Dunn’s *Locke: A Very Short Introduction* is a good guide.`,
  related: ['hobbes', 'rousseau', 'hume', 'descartes', 'mill']
},
{
  id: 'hume', name: `David Hume`, born: 1711, died: 1776, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Edinburgh; La Flèche; Paris`, role: `Philosopher, historian and essayist`,
  themes: ['knowledge', 'mind', 'god', 'ethics'], keywords: ['empiricism', 'induction', 'causation', 'habit', 'bundle theory', 'self', 'is ought', 'miracles', 'scepticism', 'treatise of human nature', 'passions', 'reason slave'],
  tagline: `Reason is, and ought only to be, the slave of the passions.`,
  summary: `Hume pushed empiricism to its limits and found that it undermined much of what we take for granted. We can’t prove the sun will rise tomorrow. We never observe causation, only one thing following another. There is no self to be found, only a bundle of perceptions. And reason can’t tell us what to value; it only serves our feelings. Yet he was the most cheerful of sceptics: when philosophy got too much, he played backgammon and dined with friends.`,
  life: `David Hume was born in Edinburgh in 1711, the younger son of a minor landowner. His father died when he was two. He went to Edinburgh University at about ten or twelve (normal then), left without a degree, and was supposed to study law. Instead he found himself obsessed with philosophy.

At about eighteen he had a breakthrough (“a new scene of thought”) and threw himself into it so hard that he had a nervous breakdown, with physical symptoms, lasting several years. He recovered partly by taking a job with a Bristol sugar merchant, then went to France, living cheaply at La Flèche, where Descartes had been educated, and wrote *A Treatise of Human Nature*. He published it in 1739–40, at 28. It “fell dead-born from the press”, he said, barely noticed.

He rewrote its ideas in more accessible form as the *Enquiries*, and wrote essays that became popular. He applied twice for professorships of philosophy, at Edinburgh and Glasgow, and was rejected both times because of his reputation for atheism. He worked as tutor to a mad young marquis, as secretary to a general on a military expedition, and as librarian of the Advocates’ Library in Edinburgh, where he wrote a six-volume *History of England* that became a bestseller and made him rich and famous.

In the 1760s he served at the British embassy in Paris, where he was lionised by the salons (they called him *le bon David*). He brought Rousseau back to England to help him escape persecution; Rousseau, increasingly paranoid, accused him of plotting against him, and a very public quarrel followed.

Hume spent his last years in Edinburgh at the centre of the Scottish Enlightenment, friends with Adam Smith. He died of bowel cancer in 1776. James Boswell visited him near the end, hoping to find the famous sceptic terrified of death. He found Hume perfectly calm, joking, and insisting he had no more fear of not existing after death than he had of not existing before birth. Boswell was disturbed. Adam Smith wrote that Hume came as near to the idea of a perfectly wise and virtuous man as human frailty will permit.`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `Impressions and ideas`, b: `Everything in the mind is either an **impression** (a vivid perception: seeing red, feeling anger) or an **idea** (a fainter copy of an impression, as in memory or imagination). Every simple idea comes from an impression. So if someone uses a word and you can’t find the impression it comes from, the word is probably meaningless. Hume used this test like a razor.` },
    { t: `The problem of induction`, b: `We believe the sun will rise tomorrow, that bread will nourish us, that fire will burn. Why? Because it always has. But how do we get from “it has always happened” to “it will happen”? Only by assuming that the future will resemble the past. And how do we know that? Because in the past, the future has always resembled the past. That is circular.

So there is no rational justification for induction. We rely on it, and must, but it is habit, not reason. Bertrand Russell’s version: the chicken that is fed every day by the farmer comes to expect food every morning, until the day the farmer wrings its neck.` },
    { t: `Causation is habit`, b: `When one billiard ball hits another and the second moves, what do we actually observe? One ball moving, contact, the other ball moving. We don’t see any “power” or “necessary connection” passing between them. After seeing this many times, our mind simply forms the habit of expecting the second event when it sees the first. The feeling of necessity is in us, not in the balls.` },
    { t: `There is no self`, b: `Look inside for your “self”, Hume said, and what do you find? “For my part, when I enter most intimately into what I call myself, I always stumble on some particular perception or other, of heat or cold, light or shade, love or hatred, pain or pleasure. I never can catch myself at any time without a perception, and never can observe anything but the perception.”

So the self is “nothing but a bundle or collection of different perceptions, which succeed each other with an inconceivable rapidity, and are in a perpetual flux and movement.” The resemblance to the Buddha’s teaching of no-self is striking; some scholars think Hume may have heard of Buddhist ideas through a Jesuit at La Flèche, but it is unproven.` },
    { t: `Reason is the slave of the passions`, b: `Reason alone can never motivate us to act. It can tell us what is true and how to get what we want, but it can’t tell us what to want. That comes from the passions: desire, aversion, love, hatred. So “reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions, and can never pretend to any other office than to serve and obey them.”

He illustrates with a deliberately shocking example: “’Tis not contrary to reason to prefer the destruction of the whole world to the scratching of my finger.” Wanting that is monstrous, but it is not a logical error.` },
    { t: `Is and ought`, b: `Hume noticed that in every book of morality he read, the author would go along describing how things are (God exists, humans have such-and-such a nature) and then suddenly start saying what we ought to do, without explaining how the “ought” follows from the “is”. It can’t, he suggested: no set of facts alone logically implies a moral conclusion. This “is–ought gap” is still one of the central problems of ethics.` },
    { t: `Morality is feeling`, b: `If morality isn’t derived from reason, where does it come from? From sentiment. We call actions virtuous when they give us a certain feeling of approval, which comes from sympathy, our natural capacity to feel what others feel. Virtue is what is useful or agreeable to ourselves or others. This made Hume a founder of both utilitarianism and modern moral psychology.` },
    { t: `On miracles`, b: `A miracle is a violation of a law of nature, and our experience in favour of the laws of nature is as complete as experience can be. So it will always be more likely that the testimony for a miracle is mistaken or false than that the miracle happened. “No testimony is sufficient to establish a miracle, unless the testimony be of such a kind that its falsehood would be more miraculous than the fact which it endeavours to establish.”` },
    { t: `Scepticism and backgammon`, b: `Hume’s scepticism was total in the study. But he noticed that it doesn’t survive dinner. At the end of Book I of the *Treatise*, after reasoning himself into a state of despair where he can’t be sure of anything, he writes: “Most fortunately it happens that since reason is incapable of dispelling these clouds, nature herself suffices to that purpose... I dine, I play a game of backgammon, I converse and am merry with my friends; and when after three or four hours’ amusement I would return to these speculations, they appear so cold, and strained, and ridiculous, that I cannot find in my heart to enter into them any farther.”

“Be a philosopher,” he wrote, “but amidst all your philosophy, be still a man.”` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `A Treatise of Human Nature`, y: `1739–40`, b: `Three books: “Of the Understanding”, “Of the Passions” and “Of Morals”. Brilliant, youthful, often difficult. Book I, Part IV (on scepticism and personal identity) is where the most famous passages are.` },
    { t: `An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding`, y: `1748`, b: `A shorter, clearer rewrite of Book I, including the essay on miracles. The best place to start.` },
    { t: `An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals`, y: `1751`, b: `Hume thought this was his best book.` },
    { t: `Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion`, y: `1779, posthumous`, b: `Three characters argue about whether we can prove God’s existence from the design of the world. Philo, the sceptic, demolishes the argument from design with arguments Darwin would later make unnecessary: maybe the world was made by a committee of gods, or by an infant god who botched it, or it simply grew, like a vegetable. Hume’s friends persuaded him not to publish it in his lifetime.` },
    { t: `My Own Life`, y: `1776`, b: `A five-page autobiography written as he was dying, calm, witty and modest.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions, and can never pretend to any other office than to serve and obey them.`, s: `*Treatise* 2.3.3` },
    { q: `When I enter most intimately into what I call myself, I always stumble on some particular perception or other.`, s: `*Treatise* 1.4.6` },
    { q: `’Tis not contrary to reason to prefer the destruction of the whole world to the scratching of my finger.`, s: `*Treatise* 2.3.3` },
    { q: `A wise man proportions his belief to the evidence.`, s: `*Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding*, section 10` },
    { q: `Be a philosopher; but, amidst all your philosophy, be still a man.`, s: `*Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding*, section 1` },
    { q: `I dine, I play a game of backgammon, I converse and am merry with my friends.`, s: `*Treatise* 1.4.7` },
    { q: `Custom, then, is the great guide of human life.`, s: `*Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding*, section 5` },
    { q: `Beauty in things exists merely in the mind which contemplates them.`, s: `*Of the Standard of Taste* (1757)` }
  ],
  fakes: [],
  critique: `Hume’s scepticism, if taken seriously, seems to undermine science itself. If induction has no rational basis, why is physics more reasonable than astrology? Philosophers have been trying to answer him ever since. Karl Popper argued that science doesn’t actually use induction: it proposes bold theories and tries to falsify them. Others think the problem is simply unsolved.

Kant said that Hume woke him from his “dogmatic slumber”, and built his whole philosophy to answer him. He argued that causation isn’t a habit we pick up from experience but a structure our minds impose on experience, which is why we can know it applies everywhere.

The bundle theory has a famous difficulty that Hume himself admitted in an appendix to the *Treatise*: what ties the bundle together? Why are these perceptions *mine*? He confessed he couldn’t answer it.

And some of his writing is ugly. A footnote to his essay “Of National Characters”, revised in 1753, says he is “apt to suspect the negroes to be naturally inferior to the whites”. It was cited by defenders of slavery. In 2020 the University of Edinburgh removed his name from one of its buildings.`,
  start: `Read *An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding*, sections 2–7 and 10 (Oxford, ed. Peter Millican). Then the *Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion*, which are genuinely entertaining. Julian Baggini’s *How the World Thinks* and James Harris’s *Hume: An Intellectual Biography* (for the ambitious) are both good. Read *My Own Life* for the man.`,
  related: ['locke', 'kant', 'buddha', 'descartes', 'mill']
},
{
  id: 'voltaire', name: `Voltaire`, born: 1694, died: 1778, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Paris; England; Ferney (near Geneva)`, role: `Writer and philosopher`,
  themes: ['politics', 'god', 'ethics', 'suffering'], keywords: ['candide', 'cultivate our garden', 'optimism', 'leibniz', 'lisbon earthquake', 'tolerance', 'ecrasez linfame', 'enlightenment', 'satire'],
  tagline: `Laughed at every kind of fanatic, and ended with: “We must cultivate our garden.”`,
  summary: `Voltaire was the Enlightenment’s great campaigner: a satirist, playwright, historian and public letter writer who spent his life attacking religious intolerance, cruelty, superstition and injustice. His short novel *Candide* demolishes the comfortable philosophy that “all is for the best” by sending its innocent hero through war, earthquake, the Inquisition and slavery. Its conclusion is modest and practical: stop theorising and work at what’s in front of you.`,
  life: `François-Marie Arouet was born in Paris in 1694, the son of a notary. Educated by Jesuits, he was brilliant and sharp-tongued from the start. He was imprisoned in the Bastille for eleven months, aged 23, for satirical verses about the Regent; there he wrote his first tragedy and adopted the pen name Voltaire. Later, after a quarrel with an aristocrat, the Chevalier de Rohan, he was beaten by Rohan’s servants in the street, imprisoned again, and allowed out only on condition he went into exile.

He spent nearly three years in England (1726–28), and it changed him. He admired English religious tolerance, freedom of speech, respect for merchants, and Newton and Locke. His *Philosophical Letters* praising England (and implicitly attacking France) was burned by the public executioner in Paris.

He lived for fifteen years with the brilliant mathematician Émilie du Châtelet at her château at Cirey, where she translated Newton’s *Principia* into French (still the standard French translation). He spent three years at the court of Frederick the Great of Prussia, which ended in a bitter quarrel. Eventually he bought an estate at Ferney, right on the French–Swiss border, so he could escape into either country if needed.

From Ferney, in his sixties and seventies, he became the most famous man in Europe. He campaigned tirelessly on cases of injustice. The best known is that of Jean Calas, a Protestant merchant in Toulouse, who was tortured and broken on the wheel in 1762 on the false charge of having murdered his son to stop him converting to Catholicism. Voltaire spent three years getting the verdict overturned. He signed his letters *Écrasez l’infâme*, “Crush the infamous thing”, meaning intolerant, persecuting religion.

He returned to Paris in 1778, at 83, to a hero’s welcome, and died there a few months later.`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `Against “all is for the best”`, b: `The German philosopher Leibniz had argued that since God is perfectly good and all-powerful, this must be the best of all possible worlds. Evils exist, but they are necessary parts of the best overall whole. Alexander Pope put it in verse: “Whatever is, is right.”

Then, on All Saints’ Day 1755, an earthquake, tsunami and fire destroyed Lisbon, killing tens of thousands of people, many of them in church at Mass. Voltaire wrote a furious poem asking whether the survivors should be told that this was for the best.

*Candide* (1759) is the fuller answer. Its hero is taught by his tutor, Dr Pangloss, that all is for the best. He is then expelled from his home, conscripted, flogged, caught in the Lisbon earthquake, nearly burned by the Inquisition, and witnesses rape, slavery and massacre. Pangloss, meanwhile, catches syphilis, loses an eye and an ear, and is hanged (not quite fatally), and still insists all is for the best. It is savage, fast and very funny.` },
    { t: `We must cultivate our garden`, b: `At the end of *Candide*, the characters, battered and disillusioned, settle on a small farm. They are bored and miserable until they meet an old Turk who tells them that he and his children cultivate their few acres, and that work keeps away three great evils: boredom, vice and need. They take up work. Pangloss still tries to explain how everything was for the best. Candide cuts him off: “That is well said, but we must cultivate our garden.”

It is deliberately ambiguous. It might mean: give up on grand metaphysical theories and do useful work. Or: retreat from a world you can’t fix and look after your own patch. Or both.` },
    { t: `Tolerance`, b: `Voltaire’s *Treatise on Tolerance* (1763), prompted by the Calas case, argues that religious persecution is not only cruel but absurd: we are all weak and liable to error, so we should pardon each other’s follies. He proposed that the right to intolerance is “absurd and barbarous: it is the right of tigers”. He wasn’t an atheist; he was a deist, believing in a creator God known by reason, not revelation. What he hated was organised religion’s claim to force its beliefs on others.` },
    { t: `Doubt and reason`, b: `“Doubt is not a pleasant condition, but certainty is an absurd one.” Voltaire’s whole career was an argument that fanaticism comes from certainty, and that reason, science, satire and free debate are its cure. “Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.”` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `Candide, or Optimism`, y: `1759`, b: `About 100 pages. One of the funniest books ever written, and one of the angriest. Read it in an evening.` },
    { t: `Philosophical Letters (Letters on the English)`, y: `1733–34`, b: `His admiring account of English politics, religion and science.` },
    { t: `Treatise on Tolerance`, y: `1763`, b: `Written in the wake of the Calas affair.` },
    { t: `Philosophical Dictionary`, y: `1764`, b: `Short, witty, alphabetical entries attacking superstition and intolerance. Pocket-sized, designed to be smuggled.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `We must cultivate our garden.`, s: `*Candide*, final line` },
    { q: `Doubt is not a pleasant condition, but certainty is an absurd one.`, s: `Letter to Frederick the Great, 28 November 1770` },
    { q: `Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.`, s: `*Questions sur les miracles* (1765), paraphrased; the original is longer` },
    { q: `If God did not exist, it would be necessary to invent him.`, s: `*Épître à l’auteur du livre des Trois imposteurs* (1770)` },
    { q: `The best is the enemy of the good.`, s: `*La Bégueule* (1772), quoting an Italian proverb` },
    { q: `Work keeps at bay three great evils: boredom, vice and need.`, s: `*Candide*, chapter 30` }
  ],
  fakes: [
    { q: `I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it.`, n: `Written by Evelyn Beatrice Hall in *The Friends of Voltaire* (1906) as a summary of his attitude. Voltaire never said it.` },
    { q: `To learn who rules over you, simply find out who you are not allowed to criticise.`, n: `Not Voltaire. It comes from a 1993 radio broadcast by the American neo-Nazi Kevin Strom. It is widely shared with Voltaire’s name on it.` }
  ],
  critique: `Voltaire was a better destroyer than builder. *Candide* is devastating as satire, but it doesn’t engage with Leibniz’s actual argument, which was more sophisticated than Pangloss’s parody. And “cultivate our garden” is a thin conclusion after so much suffering.

He also fell short of his own ideals. His writing contains ugly antisemitism, attacks on Jews that were cited by later antisemites. He invested in companies involved in the slave trade (though he also satirised slavery in *Candide*). He flattered despots like Frederick the Great and Catherine the Great when it suited him. And for all his love of reason, he was vain, vindictive and capable of petty cruelty in his many quarrels.`,
  start: `Read *Candide*. The Penguin translation by Theo Cuffe or the Oxford by Roger Pearson are both good. Then, if you want more, the *Philosophical Dictionary* or the *Treatise on Tolerance*. Ian Davidson’s *Voltaire: A Life* is readable.`,
  related: ['pascal', 'rousseau', 'hume', 'locke', 'camus']
},
{
  id: 'rousseau', name: `Jean-Jacques Rousseau`, born: 1712, died: 1778, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Geneva; Paris; Ermenonville`, role: `Philosopher and writer`,
  themes: ['politics', 'mind', 'ethics'], keywords: ['social contract', 'general will', 'man is born free', 'noble savage', 'amour propre', 'emile', 'confessions', 'inequality', 'nature'],
  tagline: `Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.`,
  summary: `Rousseau argued that civilisation, not nature, is what corrupts people. In the state of nature humans were solitary, simple and content; society brought property, inequality and *amour-propre*, the need to be seen and ranked by others, which is the source of vanity, envy and misery. His political answer, *The Social Contract*, imagines a community governed by the “general will”. It inspired the French Revolution and, some say, its terror.`,
  life: `Jean-Jacques Rousseau was born in Geneva in 1712. His mother died nine days after his birth. His father, a watchmaker, read novels with him through the night, then abandoned him at ten after a brawl. Apprenticed to an engraver who beat him, Rousseau ran away at sixteen when he found the city gates locked one evening.

He was taken in by Madame de Warens, a Catholic convert who looked after him, educated him, converted him to Catholicism and, eventually, became his lover. He called her *Maman*. He worked as a footman, music teacher, secretary and tutor, and in 1742 arrived in Paris with a new system of musical notation, which failed. He became friends with Diderot and wrote articles on music for the *Encyclopédie*.

In 1749, walking to visit Diderot, who was imprisoned at Vincennes, Rousseau read in a newspaper that the Academy of Dijon was offering a prize for an essay on whether the progress of the arts and sciences had improved morals. He was struck, he said, by a sudden illumination so intense that he sat under a tree for half an hour, weeping. He argued that civilisation had corrupted morals, and won. Suddenly he was famous.

He lived with Thérèse Levasseur, a laundress, for over thirty years, eventually marrying her. They had five children. He placed every one of them in the Paris foundling hospital at birth, where most children died. This from the man who went on to write *Émile*, the most influential book on raising children of the century.

In 1762 both *The Social Contract* and *Émile* were condemned and burned in Paris and Geneva, and he had to flee. He was stoned by villagers at Môtiers. David Hume brought him to England, but Rousseau became convinced that Hume and others were conspiring against him and fled back to France. His last years were spent in growing paranoia, writing self-justifying works. He died in 1778. In 1794 the revolutionaries moved his remains to the Panthéon in Paris.`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `Civilisation corrupts`, b: `In his *Discourse on the Origin of Inequality* (1755), Rousseau imagines humans in the state of nature: solitary, wandering, with few needs easily met, healthy, and moved by two simple feelings, self-preservation and pity at the suffering of others. Not noble savages exactly (the phrase isn’t his) but innocent, independent and content.

Then came settled life, families, agriculture, metallurgy and above all property. “The first man who, having enclosed a piece of ground, bethought himself of saying *This is mine*, and found people simple enough to believe him, was the real founder of civil society. From how many crimes, wars and murders, from how many horrors and misfortunes might not anyone have saved mankind, by pulling up the stakes, or filling up the ditch, and crying to his fellows: Beware of listening to this impostor.”` },
    { t: `Amour de soi and amour-propre`, b: `Rousseau’s sharpest psychological idea. There are two kinds of self-love. **Amour de soi** is natural and healthy: the simple desire to look after yourself. **Amour-propre** arises only in society: the need to be esteemed by others, to be seen, compared and ranked. Once people started gathering and looking at each other, “each one began to look at the others and to want to be looked at himself, and public esteem acquired a value.” The best singer, the handsomest, the strongest became the most admired.

From this comes vanity, envy, shame, contempt, and the terrible condition in which people live only in the eyes of others: “the savage lives within himself; social man, always outside himself, knows how to live only in the opinion of others.” Few philosophers have described the psychology of social media more precisely, 270 years early.` },
    { t: `The social contract and the general will`, b: `*The Social Contract* opens: “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.” How can political authority be legitimate? Rousseau’s answer: each person gives themselves wholly to the community, and in return becomes an equal member of the sovereign people. Since everyone gives the same, no one has an interest in making it burdensome for others.

The community is governed by the **general will**: not the sum of individual wishes (that is the “will of all”) but what is truly in the common interest. By obeying laws that express the general will, you obey only yourself, so you remain free. And anyone who refuses to obey the general will “shall be forced to be free”.

That last phrase is the one that has worried readers ever since.` },
    { t: `Education according to nature`, b: `*Émile* describes the education of an imaginary boy, raised in the countryside, protected from society’s corruption, learning from experience and things rather than books and words. Let children be children; don’t force adult reasoning on them; let them learn from natural consequences. It profoundly influenced modern education, from Pestalozzi and Froebel to Montessori. Book V, on the education of Émile’s future wife Sophie, is notorious for arguing that women should be educated purely to please and serve men.` },
    { t: `The Confessions`, b: `Rousseau’s autobiography begins: “I have resolved on an enterprise which has no precedent, and which, once complete, will have no imitator. My purpose is to display to my kind a portrait in every way true to nature, and the man I shall portray will be myself.” He tells everything: his masochistic pleasure at being spanked as a child, his thefts, his lies, his abandonment of his children. It is the first modern autobiography of the inner self, and in its obsession with authenticity and feeling it launched Romanticism.` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `Discourse on the Origin of Inequality`, y: `1755`, b: `Short and explosive. The best place to start.` },
    { t: `The Social Contract`, y: `1762`, b: `Four short books. Book I is the essential part.` },
    { t: `Émile, or On Education`, y: `1762`, b: `Long. Book IV contains the “Profession of Faith of the Savoyard Vicar”, a statement of natural religion that got the book burned.` },
    { t: `Julie, or the New Heloise`, y: `1761`, b: `A huge epistolary novel of passionate love that was the bestseller of the century. People wept over it.` },
    { t: `Confessions`, y: `written 1765–70, published 1782–89`, b: `The autobiography. Books I–VI, on his youth, are delightful; the later books become increasingly defensive.` },
    { t: `Reveries of the Solitary Walker`, y: `1782`, b: `Ten short, beautiful meditations written in his last years, on walking, botany and the peace of solitude. The Fifth Walk, about drifting in a boat on Lake Bienne, is one of the most lovely things he wrote.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.`, s: `*The Social Contract* I.1` },
    { q: `The first man who, having enclosed a piece of ground, bethought himself of saying “This is mine”, and found people simple enough to believe him, was the real founder of civil society.`, s: `*Discourse on Inequality*, Part II` },
    { q: `The savage lives within himself; social man, always outside himself, knows how to live only in the opinion of others.`, s: `*Discourse on Inequality*, Part II` },
    { q: `Whoever refuses to obey the general will shall be compelled to do so by the whole body. This means nothing less than that he will be forced to be free.`, s: `*The Social Contract* I.7` },
    { q: `Everything is good as it leaves the hands of the Author of things; everything degenerates in the hands of man.`, s: `*Émile*, opening line` }
  ],
  fakes: [
    { q: `Let them eat cake.`, n: `Rousseau’s *Confessions* (Book VI) reports that “a great princess” once said, of peasants with no bread, “let them eat brioche”. It is not Marie Antoinette (who was a child in Austria when he wrote it) and not Rousseau’s own line.` }
  ],
  critique: `The gap between the man and his teaching is huge. The author of the century’s great treatise on raising children abandoned all five of his own. Voltaire publicised it, cruelly and accurately. Rousseau tried to justify it in the *Confessions* (he was poor, they would be better off, it was what Plato recommended), and the justifications are painful to read.

Politically, “forced to be free” is chilling. If the general will is not what people actually want but what they *would* want if they understood their true interests, then someone has to decide what it is, and that someone may use force against the people in the name of their real freedom. Robespierre was an ardent Rousseauist. Isaiah Berlin listed Rousseau among the “enemies of human liberty”, and J. L. Talmon called his ideas “totalitarian democracy”.

His picture of natural man is also invented. Rousseau admitted it was a hypothesis, not history. But the romance of the uncorrupted primitive, and of “nature” as good and society as bad, has had a long and often silly afterlife.

And yet his diagnosis of *amour-propre*, living only in the eyes of others, is one of the most perceptive and lasting things any philosopher has written.`,
  start: `Read the *Discourse on the Origin of Inequality* (Penguin, trans. Maurice Cranston, or Oxford, trans. Franklin Philip). Then Book I of *The Social Contract*. Then the *Reveries*, for the man at his best. Leo Damrosch’s *Jean-Jacques Rousseau: Restless Genius* is an excellent biography.`,
  related: ['hobbes', 'locke', 'voltaire', 'kant', 'marx', 'thoreau']
},
{
  id: 'kant', name: `Immanuel Kant`, born: 1724, died: 1804, era: 'early-modern',
  place: `Königsberg, Prussia (now Kaliningrad, Russia)`, role: `Philosopher`,
  themes: ['ethics', 'knowledge', 'mind', 'god'], keywords: ['categorical imperative', 'critique of pure reason', 'phenomena', 'noumena', 'thing in itself', 'duty', 'autonomy', 'sapere aude', 'enlightenment', 'transcendental idealism', 'deontology'],
  tagline: `Act only on rules you could will everyone to follow, and never use a person as a mere tool.`,
  summary: `Kant tried to settle what we can know, what we ought to do, and what we may hope. He argued that the mind isn’t a passive mirror of the world: it actively structures experience through space, time and categories like causation. So we can know the world as it appears to us, but never things as they are in themselves. In ethics, morality comes from reason, not feelings or consequences: act only on principles you could will as universal laws, and treat humanity always as an end, never merely as a means.`,
  life: `Immanuel Kant was born in 1724 in Königsberg, a Baltic port city in East Prussia, the fourth of nine children of a harness maker. His parents were Pietists, members of a strict, devout Lutheran movement that stressed inner moral seriousness. He later said, of his parents, that they had given him an education that could not have been better from a moral point of view.

He studied at the University of Königsberg, worked for years as a private tutor, and then as an unsalaried lecturer, paid by the students who came to his classes, teaching up to twenty hours a week on logic, metaphysics, mathematics, physics, geography and anthropology. He was a popular, witty lecturer. He only became a full professor at 46.

He never married and never travelled more than about a hundred miles from Königsberg in his life. His routine was so regular that, according to legend, his neighbours set their clocks by his afternoon walk (the only time he was late, the story goes, was when he was absorbed in reading Rousseau’s *Émile*). He was also sociable: he held long lunches with guests nearly every day, where philosophy was banned and conversation ranged over politics, science and gossip.

Then came the “silent decade” of the 1770s, in which he published almost nothing. In 1781, at 57, he published the *Critique of Pure Reason*, one of the most important books in the history of philosophy. Over the next nine years he produced a series of masterpieces that transformed every area of philosophy.

In old age his mind declined, possibly from dementia. He died in 1804, aged 79. His last words were reportedly *Es ist gut*, “It is good”. On his tombstone are the words from the conclusion of the second *Critique*: “Two things fill the mind with ever new and increasing admiration and awe: the starry heavens above me and the moral law within me.”`,
  ideas: [
    { t: `The Copernican revolution in philosophy`, b: `Before Kant, philosophers assumed that our knowledge must conform to objects. The rationalists (Descartes, Leibniz) thought reason alone could know reality; the empiricists (Locke, Hume) thought all knowledge comes from experience. Hume had shown that experience alone can’t justify causation or induction. Kant said this woke him from his “dogmatic slumber”.

His solution was to flip the question, like Copernicus flipping the relation between the earth and the sun. What if objects must conform to our knowledge? The mind isn’t a blank slate that passively receives impressions. It actively organises experience. Space and time are the forms of our intuition, the frame in which we perceive anything at all. And the understanding applies categories (such as substance and causation) to everything we perceive. So we can know, before any particular experience, that every event we will ever experience has a cause, because that is how our minds structure experience.` },
    { t: `Phenomena and things in themselves`, b: `The price of this solution is high. We can know the world only as it appears to us, structured by our forms and categories: the world of **phenomena**. What things are like in themselves, independent of our minds (the **noumena**, or things-in-themselves), we can never know.

Imagine wearing blue-tinted glasses you can never take off. You could know with certainty that everything you ever see will look blue. But you could never know what colour things really are. This is roughly Kant’s situation, except that the “glasses” are space, time and causation themselves.

This is Kant’s “transcendental idealism”, and it shaped almost everything after him. Schopenhauer took the thing-in-itself and called it Will. Hegel tried to abolish the gap. Nietzsche mocked it.` },
    { t: `The limits of reason`, b: `When reason tries to go beyond possible experience, to answer questions about God, the soul, freedom, or whether the universe had a beginning, it gets tangled in contradictions. Kant showed that you can construct equally good arguments that the world had a beginning in time and that it didn’t. These “antinomies” show that such questions can’t be settled by theoretical reason.

So Kant demolished the traditional proofs of God’s existence. But he said he did this “to make room for faith”. We can’t prove God, freedom or immortality, but we can’t disprove them either, and morality gives us reason to hope for them.` },
    { t: `Good will`, b: `The *Groundwork* opens: “It is impossible to think of anything at all in the world, or indeed even beyond it, that could be considered good without limitation except a good will.” Intelligence, courage, wealth, even happiness can all be used for evil. Only a good will is good in itself. It shines “like a jewel” even if it achieves nothing.

And a good will is one that acts from duty: doing the right thing because it is right, not because it benefits you or because you happen to feel like it. The shopkeeper who gives correct change only because honesty is good for business isn’t acting from duty.` },
    { t: `The categorical imperative`, b: `A hypothetical imperative says: if you want X, do Y. (If you want to be healthy, exercise.) A **categorical** imperative says: do Y, full stop, regardless of what you want. Moral rules are categorical. Kant gives several formulations of the one supreme principle of morality. The two most famous:

**The formula of universal law:** “Act only according to that maxim through which you can at the same time will that it become a universal law.” Before you act, ask: what if everyone did this? If you make a false promise to borrow money, your maxim is: when I need money, I’ll promise to repay it knowing I won’t. If everyone did that, promising would become meaningless and no one would lend money on a promise. The maxim defeats itself. So it’s wrong.

**The formula of humanity:** “So act that you use humanity, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, always at the same time as an end, never merely as a means.” Every rational being has dignity, not a price. You may use a shopkeeper as a means (to buy bread), but not *merely* as a means; you must respect them as a person with their own ends. Lying, manipulation, coercion and exploitation all treat people as mere tools.

The second formula is behind the modern idea of human rights and human dignity.` },
    { t: `Autonomy`, b: `Morality isn’t imposed from outside, by God, society or nature. It comes from our own reason. A rational being gives the moral law to itself. That is autonomy, literally “self-law”. Following your desires is actually heteronomy: being ruled by something outside your rational will. Only when you act from reason are you truly free.` },
    { t: `Dare to know`, b: `In a short 1784 essay, “What is Enlightenment?”, Kant defined it as humanity’s emergence from its self-incurred immaturity, meaning the inability to use your own understanding without guidance from someone else. Its motto: *Sapere aude!* “Have the courage to use your own understanding!” It is so comfortable to be immature, he says. If I have a book that thinks for me, a pastor who has a conscience for me, a doctor who judges my diet for me, I don’t need to think at all.

Replace “book” with “algorithm” and the essay is as sharp as ever.` }
  ],
  works: [
    { t: `Critique of Pure Reason`, y: `1781; 2nd ed. 1787`, b: `His masterpiece on the limits of knowledge. It is famously difficult, around 800 pages of dense, technical German. Almost no one should start here. The prefaces to both editions are readable and set out the Copernican idea.` },
    { t: `Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals`, y: `1785`, b: `His ethics in under 100 pages, introducing the categorical imperative. Challenging but manageable, and the right starting point.` },
    { t: `Critique of Practical Reason`, y: `1788`, b: `Ethics in more detail, including the argument that morality requires us to postulate freedom, God and immortality. Ends with the starry heavens.` },
    { t: `Critique of Judgement`, y: `1790`, b: `On beauty, the sublime and purpose in nature. Hugely influential on aesthetics and Romanticism.` },
    { t: `What is Enlightenment? and Perpetual Peace`, y: `1784; 1795`, b: `Two short, readable essays. *Perpetual Peace* proposes a federation of free states to end war, an idea that influenced the League of Nations and the United Nations.` }
  ],
  quotes: [
    { q: `Two things fill the mind with ever new and increasing admiration and awe, the more often and steadily we reflect upon them: the starry heavens above me and the moral law within me.`, s: `*Critique of Practical Reason*, Conclusion` },
    { q: `Act only according to that maxim through which you can at the same time will that it become a universal law.`, s: `*Groundwork*, section 2` },
    { q: `So act that you use humanity, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, always at the same time as an end, never merely as a means.`, s: `*Groundwork*, section 2` },
    { q: `Sapere aude! Have courage to use your own understanding!`, s: `“What is Enlightenment?” (1784)` },
    { q: `Out of the crooked timber of humanity, no straight thing was ever made.`, s: `“Idea for a Universal History”, sixth proposition (1784)` },
    { q: `Thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind.`, s: `*Critique of Pure Reason*, A51/B75` },
    { q: `I had to deny knowledge in order to make room for faith.`, s: `*Critique of Pure Reason*, Preface to the 2nd edition, Bxxx` }
  ],
  fakes: [
    { q: `Science is organised knowledge. Wisdom is organised life.`, n: `Not Kant. The line appears in Will Durant’s *The Story of Philosophy*, in his chapter on Kant, as Durant’s own summary.` },
    { q: `We are not rich by what we possess but by what we can do without.`, n: `Frequently attributed to Kant, with no known source in his works.` }
  ],
  critique: `The most famous objection to Kant’s ethics is the murderer at the door. A friend is hiding in your house; a murderer knocks and asks where they are. May you lie? Kant, in a short 1797 essay, said no: lying is always wrong, even here. Almost everyone thinks this is absurd, and many think it shows the rigidity of a rule-based ethics that ignores consequences.

The universal law test also has problems. Some wrong actions pass it if you describe the maxim cleverly enough, and some harmless ones fail it (“I will buy clothes but never sell them” couldn’t be universalised). Hegel complained that the formula is empty: it tells you to be consistent, but not what to be consistent about.

Critics also find Kant’s moral psychology cold. Bernard Williams imagined a man who could save either his wife or a stranger from drowning. If he stops to think “it is permissible for me to save my wife”, he has “one thought too many”; he should simply save her because she is his wife. Love, loyalty and feeling seem to have no proper place in Kant’s account of moral worth.

And the thing-in-itself is puzzling. If we can know nothing about it, how does Kant know it exists, or that it causes our experiences? His own categories, including causation, are only supposed to apply to appearances.

Like Hume, Kant also wrote racist passages in his lectures on anthropology and geography, ranking races by capacity. Scholars debate how central this is to his thought. It sits uncomfortably next to a moral theory built on the equal dignity of every rational being.`,
  start: `Don’t start with the *Critique of Pure Reason*. Start with the essay “What is Enlightenment?” (ten pages), then the *Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals* (Cambridge, trans. Mary Gregor; or the Oxford edition by Thomas E. Hill and Arnulf Zweig with good notes). Roger Scruton’s *Kant: A Very Short Introduction* is clear. For the first *Critique*, Sebastian Gardner’s *Kant and the Critique of Pure Reason* is the best guide.`,
  related: ['hume', 'rousseau', 'hegel', 'schopenhauer', 'descartes', 'arendt']
}
);
